  abstract={Most of the real-world applications of these multimodal systems face severe instability and degradation in performance. when one or both of the modalities are missing, noisy, or unreliable. Most of the movie genre classification models developed so far operate Far assume full availability of modalities, and often suffer It suffers from significant accuracy drops if the input is incomplete. This challenge is overcome by an approach that we introduce by: Effectively incorporates movie poster images with plot summaries. The proposed approach leverages a pre-trained Vision transformers to extract the visual features, while a T5 encoder of the textual representations, followed by a fully connected fusion layer that merges the streams of features. The model is trained on the MM-IMDb dataset, that is quite challenging due to the diversity of genre categories, the imbalance in genre categories. Improvement by In order to achieve robustness in conditions where there is incomplete input, regularization techniques are added and missing-modality conditions are simulated during training. We also apply contrastive alignment to maintain semantic consistency between visual and textual features. The fused architecture outperforms all single-modality baselines: the The micro F1 score is 0.69, and the mean Average Precision amounts to to 0.7. These results confirm that our framework can handle -which means incomplete or inconsistent inputs well, which is promising for real-world applications where one cannot always be sure of the Quality of the input data in multimedia.},
